/* Fun facts for the home page (Geography). Indonesian versions, in the same order, are in src/lang/id/facts.js. */
const FACTS = [
  { t: `Indonesia is wider than the United States`, b: `From Sabang in the far west to Merauke in the east, Indonesia stretches more than 5,000 km, longer than the width of the mainland United States. Its thousands of islands lie across three time zones: WIB, WITA and WIT. Most of the country is sea, and its waters join the Pacific and Indian Oceans.` },
  { t: `More than 17,000 islands`, b: `Indonesia is the world's largest archipelagic country, with around 17,000 islands. Counting them is surprisingly hard: sandbanks appear and disappear, and at high tide some islands split into two. In recent years Indonesia has formally registered the names of its islands with the United Nations, and the official count has changed as surveys improved.` },
  { t: `The eruption that cancelled summer`, b: `The eruption of Mount Tambora on Sumbawa in April 1815 was the largest in recorded history. Its ash and sulphur cooled the whole planet, and 1816 became the "Year Without a Summer" in Europe and North America, with snow in June and failed harvests. Stuck indoors by the gloomy weather at Lake Geneva that summer, Mary Shelley began writing Frankenstein.` },
  { t: `Lake Toba is a giant volcanic crater`, b: `Lake Toba in North Sumatra fills a caldera left by a supereruption about 74,000 years ago, one of the largest volcanic eruptions of the last few million years. The lake is about 100 km long, making it the largest volcanic lake in the world. Samosir Island in its middle rose slowly after the eruption as magma pushed the floor back up.` },
  { t: `The child of Krakatoa`, b: `After Krakatoa blew itself apart in 1883, a new volcano, Anak Krakatau ("Child of Krakatoa"), broke the sea surface in 1927 and grew into an island. In December 2018 part of its flank collapsed into the sea and caused a tsunami that struck the coasts of Banten and Lampung without warning, because it was not triggered by an earthquake.` },
  { t: `An invisible line between Bali and Lombok`, b: `In 1859 the naturalist Alfred Russel Wallace noticed that the animals of Bali are Asian (monkeys, tigers once lived there) while those of Lombok, only 35 km away, are more Australian (cockatoos, marsupials further east). The deep Lombok Strait never became dry land during the ice ages, so animals could not cross. The boundary is called the Wallace Line.` },
  { t: `Glaciers on the equator`, b: `Puncak Jaya in Papua, at about 4,884 m, is the highest mountain on any island in the world and the highest peak between the Himalayas and the Andes. Although it lies just 4° south of the equator, it has small tropical glaciers. They are melting fast and scientists expect them to disappear within the next few years.` },
  { t: `The richest reefs on Earth`, b: `Raja Ampat in Southwest Papua lies in the heart of the Coral Triangle and has the highest recorded marine biodiversity on the planet. Surveys have found more than 1,500 species of reef fish and roughly three quarters of all known hard coral species in its waters. The Coral Triangle spans Indonesia, the Philippines, Malaysia, Papua New Guinea, Timor-Leste and the Solomon Islands.` },
  { t: `Dragons live only in Indonesia`, b: `The Komodo dragon, the largest living lizard, can grow to about 3 m long and 70 kg. In the wild it lives only on Komodo, Rinca, Flores and a few small nearby islands in East Nusa Tenggara. Komodo National Park was listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1991 to protect it.` },
  { t: `One island, three countries`, b: `Borneo is the third-largest island in the world and the only island shared by three countries: Indonesia (Kalimantan), Malaysia (Sabah and Sarawak) and Brunei. Its rainforests, among the oldest on Earth, are home to orangutans and the rafflesia, the world's largest single flower. New Guinea, shared by Indonesia and Papua New Guinea, is the second-largest island.` },
  { t: `The day shadows disappear in Pontianak`, b: `The equator runs right through the city of Pontianak in West Kalimantan, marked by the Khatulistiwa Monument. Around the equinoxes in March and September, the Sun at noon is directly overhead, and upright objects cast almost no shadow. The city celebrates this "culmination day" every year.` },
  { t: `Why Indonesia rarely gets typhoons`, b: `Tropical cyclones need the Coriolis effect, caused by the Earth's rotation, to start spinning. That effect is zero at the equator and very weak near it, so cyclones almost never form within about 5° of the equator. Most of Indonesia lies inside that band, which is why typhoons usually pass to the north (towards the Philippines) or the south (towards Australia).` },
  { t: `Jakarta is sinking`, b: `Parts of northern Jakarta have been sinking by up to about 25 cm a year, mainly because groundwater is pumped out faster than it is replaced and the soft ground compacts. Large areas already lie below sea level, protected by sea walls. This is one reason Indonesia decided in 2022 to build a new capital, Nusantara, in East Kalimantan.` },
  { t: `The world's biggest city may be Jakarta`, b: `Comparing city sizes is tricky because every country draws city boundaries differently. In 2025 a United Nations report used one consistent definition of a city for the whole world and ranked the Jakarta urban area first, with about 42 million people, ahead of Dhaka and Tokyo. Earlier rankings, using national definitions, had put Tokyo at the top.` },
  { t: `Borobudur is the world's largest Buddhist temple`, b: `Built in Central Java in the 8th–9th century, Borobudur is the largest Buddhist temple in the world. It is decorated with 2,672 relief panels and 504 Buddha statues, and pilgrims walk around its terraces clockwise, climbing symbolically from the everyday world to enlightenment. It was buried under volcanic ash for centuries and became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1991.` },
  { t: `Hundreds of languages in one country`, b: `Indonesia is home to more than 700 living languages, second only to Papua New Guinea, which has about 840. Bahasa Indonesia, based on Malay, was chosen as the language of unity in the Youth Pledge (Sumpah Pemuda) of 1928, so that people from different islands could understand each other while keeping their own regional languages.` },
  { t: `Everest would vanish in the Mariana Trench`, b: `The Challenger Deep in the Mariana Trench in the western Pacific is about 10,935 m deep, the deepest known point in the ocean. If Mount Everest were placed there, its summit would still be more than 2 km under water. Only a few people have reached the bottom: first Jacques Piccard and Don Walsh in 1960, and film director James Cameron alone in 2012.` },
  { t: `Everest is still growing`, b: `In 2020 China and Nepal jointly announced a new height for Mount Everest: 8,848.86 m. The mountain is still rising by a few millimetres a year because the Indian plate keeps pushing north into Asia, although earthquakes can lower it slightly. Its local names are Sagarmatha in Nepal and Chomolungma in Tibet.` },
  { t: `The tallest mountain is not Everest`, b: `Everest has the highest summit above sea level, but Mauna Kea in Hawai'i is taller when measured from its base. Most of it is hidden under the Pacific: from the ocean floor to the summit it is about 10,200 m tall, more than a kilometre taller than Everest. Its dry, calm summit air makes it one of the best places on Earth for telescopes.` },
  { t: `The closest point to the stars`, b: `Because the Earth bulges at the equator, the summit of Chimborazo in Ecuador, only 6,263 m above sea level, is the point on Earth's surface farthest from the planet's centre, about 2 km farther than the top of Everest. Standing there, you are the closest you can get to outer space while standing on land.` },
  { t: `The Earth is slightly squashed`, b: `The Earth is not a perfect sphere. Its rotation makes it bulge at the equator, so the diameter across the equator is about 43 km larger than from pole to pole. Isaac Newton predicted this flattening in 1687, and French expeditions to Lapland and Peru in the 1730s measured it and proved him right.` },
  { t: `The Amazon carries a fifth of the world's river water`, b: `The Amazon discharges about 200,000 cubic metres of water into the Atlantic every second, roughly a fifth of all the river water that reaches the oceans. Its outflow is so strong that the sea stays fresh enough to drink more than 100 km offshore. Whether the Nile or the Amazon is the longest river depends on where you decide the source is.` },
  { t: `Russia has eleven time zones`, b: `Russia is the largest country in the world, covering about 17 million km², and spans 11 time zones. When it is breakfast time in Kaliningrad on the Baltic Sea, it is already evening on the Kamchatka Peninsula. Its area is even slightly larger than the surface area of the dwarf planet Pluto.` },
  { t: `Antarctica is the biggest desert`, b: `A desert is defined by low precipitation, not by heat. Antarctica receives so little snow inland that it is the largest desert on Earth, about 14 million km². Its ice sheet holds about 90% of all the ice on the planet and around 70% of the world's fresh water; if it all melted, sea level would rise by roughly 58 m.` },
  { t: `The driest place is not the Sahara`, b: `Parts of the Atacama Desert in Chile are the driest non-polar places on Earth. Some weather stations there have gone years without recording any rain at all. The soil is so dry and lifeless that NASA has tested Mars rovers there, yet after a rare rainfall the desert can burst into flower.` },
  { t: `The Sahara used to be green`, b: `Between about 11,000 and 5,000 years ago, a shift in the Earth's orbit strengthened the African monsoon and the Sahara was covered with grassland, lakes and rivers. Rock paintings deep in today's desert show giraffes, hippos and cattle herders. The Sahara is now about 9 million km², roughly the size of the United States.` },
  { t: `The continents were once one`, b: `In 1912 Alfred Wegener proposed that the continents had once formed a single supercontinent, Pangaea, which broke apart about 200 million years ago. He pointed to matching coastlines, rocks and fossils on opposite sides of the Atlantic, but most geologists rejected the idea. Only in the 1960s, with the discovery of seafloor spreading, did plate tectonics prove him right.` },
  { t: `Continents move as fast as fingernails grow`, b: `The Atlantic Ocean widens by a few centimetres every year as new sea floor forms along the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, about as fast as your fingernails grow. Satellite positioning can now measure plate motions directly. Over 100 million years, a few centimetres a year adds up to thousands of kilometres.` },
  { t: `Sea shells on top of Everest`, b: `The summit of Mount Everest is made of limestone containing fossils of sea creatures such as trilobites and crinoids. The rock formed on the floor of an ancient ocean, the Tethys, more than 400 million years ago, and was lifted almost 9 km as India collided with Asia. The Himalayas are among the youngest great mountain ranges on Earth.` },
  { t: `Diving between two continents`, b: `Iceland sits on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, where the North American and Eurasian plates are pulling apart. In the Silfra fissure in Þingvellir National Park, divers can swim through crystal-clear glacial water in the crack between the two plates, and in places touch both sides at once.` },
  { t: `The Earth's core is as hot as the Sun's surface`, b: `The Earth's inner core is a ball of mostly iron and nickel about 2,400 km across, at a temperature of roughly 5,000–6,000 °C, similar to the surface of the Sun. It stays solid despite the heat because the pressure there is more than three million times the air pressure at the surface. Its heat drives the flow of the liquid outer core that creates Earth's magnetic field.` },
  { t: `The deepest hole ever dug`, b: `The Kola Superdeep Borehole in northern Russia reached 12,262 m in 1989, still the deepest artificial point on Earth. At the bottom the rock was about 180 °C, much hotter than expected, and drilling had to stop. Even so, it penetrated only about a third of the way through the continental crust, and just 0.2% of the way to the Earth's centre.` },
  { t: `We have better maps of Mars than of our seabed`, b: `Oceans cover about 71% of the Earth, but only about a quarter of the sea floor has been mapped in high detail with ship-based sonar. The surfaces of the Moon and Mars are mapped more completely, because we can photograph them directly. An international project, Seabed 2030, aims to map the whole ocean floor by 2030.` },
  { t: `Half your oxygen comes from the ocean`, b: `Tiny floating algae and bacteria called phytoplankton produce roughly half of the oxygen in Earth's atmosphere through photosynthesis. One of them, Prochlorococcus, is thought to be the most abundant photosynthetic organism on the planet. So every second breath you take you owe to the ocean, not to forests.` },
  { t: `Lake Baikal holds a fifth of the world's lake water`, b: `Lake Baikal in Siberia is the deepest lake in the world (1,642 m) and one of the oldest, formed about 25–30 million years ago in a rift where the crust is pulling apart. It holds roughly 20% of the unfrozen fresh surface water on Earth, more than all five North American Great Lakes combined, and is home to the nerpa, the only freshwater seal.` },
  { t: `The Dead Sea is the lowest land on Earth`, b: `The shore of the Dead Sea, between Jordan and Israel, lies about 430 m below sea level, the lowest land on Earth. Its water is almost ten times saltier than the ocean, so swimmers float effortlessly. The lake is shrinking by more than a metre each year because the river that feeds it is diverted for farming and drinking water.` },
  { t: `The smallest country in the world`, b: `Vatican City, inside Rome, covers only about 0.44 km², smaller than many city parks, and has a population of a few hundred. It is the headquarters of the Catholic Church and issues its own stamps and coins. Monaco, the second smallest, is about 2 km² but is one of the most densely populated countries on Earth.` },
  { t: `Greenland is not as big as it looks`, b: `On the familiar Mercator world map, Greenland looks about as big as Africa. In reality Africa is about 14 times larger. Gerardus Mercator designed his projection in 1569 for sailors, because it keeps compass directions straight, but it stretches areas more and more toward the poles.` },
  { t: `Africa is enormous`, b: `Africa covers about 30 million km². The United States, China, India and most of Europe could all fit inside it at the same time. Because world maps usually shrink the tropics compared with high latitudes, most people greatly underestimate its size.` },
  { t: `Measuring the Earth with a stick`, b: `Around 240 BCE Eratosthenes, librarian of Alexandria, learned that at noon on midsummer's day the Sun shone straight down a well in Syene (Aswan), while in Alexandria a stick cast a shadow at about 7°. Since 7° is about one fiftieth of a circle, he multiplied the distance between the cities by 50 and got a circumference of the Earth remarkably close to the true 40,000 km.` },
  { t: `GPS needs four satellites`, b: `A GPS receiver works out its position by timing radio signals from satellites about 20,000 km above the Earth. Signals from three satellites fix a point in space, and a fourth is needed to correct the receiver's own clock. The American GPS system has about 30 active satellites, so at least four are almost always in view from anywhere on Earth.` },
  { t: `Point Nemo, the loneliest place on Earth`, b: `Point Nemo in the South Pacific is the spot in the ocean farthest from any land: about 2,700 km from the nearest islands. Often the nearest humans are the astronauts on the International Space Station passing overhead about 400 km up. Space agencies use the area as a "spacecraft cemetery" for old satellites and space stations.` },
  { t: `The lightning capital of the world`, b: `Lake Maracaibo in Venezuela has more lightning per square kilometre than anywhere else on Earth. On about 140 to 160 nights a year, warm moist air from the lake meets cool winds from the Andes and produces almost continuous thunderstorms, the "Catatumbo lightning". Sailors once used its flashes as a natural lighthouse.` },
  { t: `Hottest and coldest temperatures ever recorded`, b: `The highest air temperature officially recognised by the World Meteorological Organization is 56.7 °C, at Furnace Creek in Death Valley, California, in July 1913. The lowest is −89.2 °C, at Russia's Vostok Station in Antarctica in July 1983. Satellites have detected even colder surface temperatures, below −90 °C, on the high Antarctic plateau.` },
  { t: `The wettest places on Earth`, b: `Mawsynram and Cherrapunji, villages in the hills of Meghalaya in north-east India, receive around 11,000–12,000 mm of rain a year, among the highest averages on Earth. Monsoon winds from the Bay of Bengal are forced up the steep hills and drop their moisture. Local people grow "living root bridges" from the roots of rubber fig trees that survive the floods.` },
  { t: `Eight billion people`, b: `According to the United Nations, the world population passed 8 billion on 15 November 2022. It took about 200,000 years of human history to reach 1 billion around 1804, but only 12 years to go from 7 to 8 billion. In 2023 India overtook China as the most populous country, and Indonesia is fourth, after the United States.` },
  { t: `The Great Barrier Reef is the largest living structure`, b: `Australia's Great Barrier Reef stretches about 2,300 km and is made of around 2,900 individual reefs built by billions of tiny coral polyps. It is the largest structure made by living things and can be seen from space. Rising sea temperatures have caused several mass coral bleaching events there since 2016.` },
  { t: `You cannot see the Great Wall from the Moon`, b: `A popular myth says the Great Wall of China is the only human-made structure visible from the Moon. In fact, it is far too narrow, at most about 9 m wide, to be seen even from low Earth orbit with the naked eye, let alone from the Moon 384,000 km away. Astronauts say city lights at night are much easier to spot.` },
  { t: `The highest tides in the world`, b: `In the Bay of Fundy in Canada, the difference between high and low tide can reach about 16 m, the height of a four-storey building. The bay's shape and length make the water slosh back and forth in time with the tides, amplifying them. With each tide about 160 billion tonnes of seawater flow in and out.` },
  { t: `The ozone layer is healing`, b: `In the 1980s scientists discovered a hole in the ozone layer over Antarctica caused by CFC gases from sprays and fridges. The 1987 Montreal Protocol, later ratified by every country in the world, phased out these chemicals. The United Nations expects the ozone layer to recover to 1980 levels by around 2066 over Antarctica, a rare global environmental success.` },
  { t: `Hurricane, typhoon or cyclone?`, b: `Hurricanes, typhoons and cyclones are the same kind of storm: a rotating tropical low-pressure system with winds of at least 119 km/h. They are called hurricanes in the Atlantic and north-east Pacific, typhoons in the north-west Pacific, and cyclones in the Indian Ocean and around Australia. They spin anticlockwise in the Northern Hemisphere and clockwise in the Southern.` },
  { t: `A day is not exactly 24 hours`, b: `The Earth turns once relative to the stars in about 23 hours 56 minutes, a sidereal day. Because it also moves along its orbit, it must turn a little more before the Sun returns to the same place in the sky, which makes the solar day 24 hours. That is why the stars rise about four minutes earlier each night.` },
  { t: `A city on two continents`, b: `Istanbul in Türkiye spreads across both sides of the Bosphorus Strait, so part of the city is in Europe and part in Asia. Commuters cross between continents every day by bridge, ferry and an undersea rail tunnel. As Byzantium and later Constantinople, it was a capital of empires for more than 1,500 years.` },
  { t: `The river that flows north`, b: `The Nile, one of the longest rivers in the world, flows northward for more than 6,600 km from East Africa to the Mediterranean Sea. For thousands of years its yearly flood left fertile silt along its banks, allowing ancient Egyptian civilisation to grow in the middle of a desert. Since 1970 the Aswan High Dam has controlled the flood.` },
  { t: `Canada has the longest coastline`, b: `Canada has the longest coastline of any country, more than 200,000 km including its tens of thousands of islands, enough to go round the equator about six times. Measuring a coastline is tricky, though: the result depends on the scale of the map, which is why different sources give different figures for countries such as Indonesia and Norway.` },
  { t: `Where does the name Everest come from?`, b: `Surveyors of the British Great Trigonometrical Survey of India measured Peak XV in the 1850s and found it to be the highest in the world. In 1865 it was named after George Everest, a former Surveyor General, even though he objected and had never seen the mountain. His own name was actually pronounced "Eve-rest".` },
  { t: `Brazil borders almost every South American country`, b: `Brazil covers nearly half of South America and shares a border with every country on the continent except Chile and Ecuador. It is the fifth-largest country in the world and contains about 60% of the Amazon rainforest. Portuguese is spoken there because Brazil was a Portuguese colony, while most of its neighbours speak Spanish.` },
  { t: `Why the sea is salty`, b: `Rain is slightly acidic and slowly dissolves minerals from rocks on land. Rivers carry these dissolved salts to the sea, and when seawater evaporates the water leaves but the salt stays behind. Over hundreds of millions of years the oceans have reached about 3.5% salt, mostly sodium chloride. Undersea volcanic vents add more minerals.` },
  { t: `Most of Earth's fresh water is frozen or hidden`, b: `Only about 2.5% of all water on Earth is fresh, and most of that is locked in ice sheets and glaciers or stored underground. Rivers and lakes, the water we see and use most, hold well under 1% of the world's fresh water. That is why protecting groundwater and catchments is so important for drinking water.` },
  { t: `The Ring of Fire`, b: `Around the edges of the Pacific Ocean lies a 40,000 km horseshoe of volcanoes and earthquake zones called the Ring of Fire. About three quarters of the world's active volcanoes and around 90% of its earthquakes occur there, where oceanic plates dive beneath other plates. Indonesia, Japan, the Philippines and Chile all sit on it.` },
  { t: `Living with 127 volcanoes`, b: `Indonesia has about 127 active volcanoes, and more people live close to active volcanoes there than in any other country. Volcanic ash breaks down into very fertile soil, which is one reason Java can support such a dense population. Indonesia's volcano agency constantly monitors the most dangerous ones, such as Merapi and Semeru.` },
  { t: `The Indian Ocean tsunami of 2004`, b: `On 26 December 2004 a magnitude 9.1 earthquake off the west coast of Sumatra, one of the largest ever recorded, lifted the seabed along a fault about 1,300 km long. The tsunami it caused killed about 230,000 people in 14 countries, most of them in Aceh. Afterwards, an Indian Ocean tsunami warning system was built so that coasts can be warned within minutes.` },
  { t: `A day on Earth is getting longer`, b: `The Moon's gravity raises tides, and the friction of the tides acts like a brake on the Earth's spin. Fossil corals from about 400 million years ago show daily growth rings that suggest a year then had about 400 days, each only about 22 hours long. Today the day still lengthens by roughly 2 milliseconds per century.` },
  { t: `There are 195 countries`, b: `Most lists count 195 countries: the 193 member states of the United Nations plus the two observer states, the Holy See (Vatican City) and Palestine. The newest UN member is South Sudan, which became independent in 2011. Timor-Leste, Indonesia's neighbour, joined the UN in 2002.` },
  { t: `Why the tropics have wet and dry seasons`, b: `Near the equator the Sun is high all year, so temperatures change little. Instead, seasons are set by the Intertropical Convergence Zone, a belt of rising air and heavy rain that shifts north and south with the Sun. In much of Indonesia the monsoon winds bring the rainy season from about October to April and a drier season from about May to September.` },
  { t: `Mangroves protect coasts`, b: `Mangrove forests grow in salty tidal mud along tropical coasts, and Indonesia has more mangroves than any other country, about a fifth of the world's total. Their tangled roots slow waves and storm surges, trap sediment and shelter young fish. Mangroves also store several times more carbon per hectare than most tropical forests on land.` },
  { t: `Rivers in the sky`, b: `Long, narrow bands of water vapour called atmospheric rivers carry moisture from the tropics toward higher latitudes. An average one carries about as much water as the Mississippi River pours into the sea, and the strongest carry up to 15 times as much; when one hits mountains it can release days of heavy rain. They supply much of the rain and snow on the west coast of the United States.` },
  { t: `Indonesia has more sea than land`, b: `Indonesia's land area is about 1.9 million km², but including its territorial waters and Exclusive Economic Zone, its sea area is well over three times as large. The Djuanda Declaration of 1957 claimed the waters between the islands as Indonesian, an idea that became the "archipelagic state" principle of the UN Convention on the Law of the Sea in 1982.` },
  { t: `Indonesia has one of the world's longest coastlines`, b: `Surrounded by some 17,000 islands, Indonesia's coastline is among the longest of any country; the national mapping agency gives a figure of about 99,000 km. Depending on how coastlines are measured, usually only Canada, and sometimes Norway, is ranked higher. Much of the population lives near the coast, depending on fishing, trade and ports.` },
  { t: `Java is the most populous island on Earth`, b: `Java holds more than 150 million people, over half of Indonesia's population, on an island about the size of England. Its volcanic soils are extremely fertile, which has supported dense farming populations for centuries. Honshu in Japan, the next most populous island, has roughly 100 million people.` },
  { t: `The Indonesian Throughflow`, b: `The only place where warm water from the Pacific flows into the Indian Ocean at the tropics is through the Indonesian seas, especially the Makassar Strait and the Lombok and Ombai straits. This "Indonesian Throughflow" carries roughly 15 million cubic metres of water per second and helps shape the climate of Australia, Asia and even the Atlantic.` },
  { t: `The deepest point in Indonesian waters`, b: `The Banda Sea is one of the deepest seas in Indonesia, with the Weber Deep reaching about 7,200 m. It is the deepest point in the world's oceans that is not in a trench. The nearby Java (Sunda) Trench, south of Java and Sumatra, plunges to about 7,450 m, marking where the Indo-Australian Plate slides under Eurasia.` },
  { t: `Sulawesi's strange shape`, b: `Sulawesi looks like a letter K because it was assembled from several pieces of crust that collided over millions of years. Its isolation between the Asian and Australian regions produced unique animals such as the anoa (a dwarf buffalo), the babirusa ("pig-deer") and the maleo bird, which buries its eggs in volcanically heated sand.` },
  { t: `Wallacea, a region of its own`, b: `Between the Wallace Line and the Lydekker Line lies Wallacea: Sulawesi, Maluku and Nusa Tenggara. These islands were never joined to either Asia or Australia, so their animals arrived by crossing the sea and evolved in isolation. Wallacea has an unusually high share of endemic species found nowhere else.` },
  { t: `Sundaland was once dry land`, b: `During the last ice age, about 20,000 years ago, sea level was around 120 m lower than today. Sumatra, Java, Borneo and the Malay Peninsula were joined in one great landmass called Sundaland, and animals such as tigers, elephants and orangutans could walk between them. Rising seas flooded the Java Sea and the Karimata Strait.` },
  { t: `Flores was home to "hobbits"`, b: `In 2003 scientists found remains of a tiny human species, Homo floresiensis, in Liang Bua cave on Flores. Adults stood only about 1.1 m tall. They lived on the island until at least around 50,000 years ago, alongside dwarf elephants (Stegodon) and giant rats, showing how island isolation can shrink or enlarge animals.` },
  { t: `Java Man`, b: `In 1891 the Dutch doctor Eugène Dubois found fossils at Trinil on the Solo River in East Java, the first known remains of Homo erectus, nicknamed "Java Man". Later finds at Sangiran, now a UNESCO World Heritage Site, have made Central Java one of the most important places on Earth for understanding human evolution.` },
  { t: `An island in a lake on an island`, b: `Samosir, the island in the middle of Lake Toba, is about 630 km², roughly the size of Singapore. It is an island within a lake on an island, and it has its own lakes too. The Batak people live around the lake, and its caldera has been recognised as a UNESCO Global Geopark.` },
  { t: `The coloured lakes of Kelimutu`, b: `At the summit of Mount Kelimutu on Flores lie three crater lakes that change colour, from turquoise to green, red, brown and black, sometimes within months. The colours depend on dissolved minerals and on volcanic gases that change the oxidation state of iron and manganese in the water. Local tradition says the lakes are resting places for souls.` },
  { t: `Blue fire at Kawah Ijen`, b: `At night the Ijen crater in East Java glows with eerie blue flames. They come from sulfur-rich volcanic gases escaping at high temperature and burning in air; some of the gas condenses into liquid sulfur that flows and burns too. Ijen also holds the world's largest highly acidic crater lake, with a pH below 0.5.` },
  { t: `Mud volcano in Sidoarjo`, b: `Since May 2006, hot mud has been erupting at Sidoarjo in East Java, in what became known as the Lapindo or Lusi mud flow. At its peak it poured out up to 180,000 cubic metres a day, burying villages and displacing tens of thousands of people. Scientists still debate how much a nearby gas well and a recent earthquake each contributed to triggering it.` },
  { t: `Merapi, the "mountain of fire"`, b: `Mount Merapi on the border of Central Java and Yogyakarta is one of the most active volcanoes on Earth, erupting every few years. Its eruptions often produce pyroclastic flows called "wedhus gembel". The 2010 eruption killed more than 300 people, including the mountain's famous spiritual guardian Mbah Maridjan, and forced hundreds of thousands to evacuate.` },
  { t: `The earthquake that moved an island`, b: `The magnitude 9.1 earthquake of December 2004 off Sumatra shifted parts of the seafloor by more than 10 metres, lifted some islands such as Simeulue by over a metre and slightly changed the Earth's rotation, shortening the day by a few microseconds. The fault ruptured for about 10 minutes, one of the longest ruptures ever recorded.` },
  { t: `Palu 2018: when the ground flowed`, b: `The magnitude 7.5 earthquake near Palu, Central Sulawesi, in September 2018 caused a tsunami in Palu Bay and also liquefaction: waterlogged sandy soil turned to a thick liquid and whole neighbourhoods such as Petobo and Balaroa slid and sank. More than 4,000 people died or went missing.` },
  { t: `Earthquake early warning uses the speed of waves`, b: `An earthquake sends out fast P-waves and slower, more destructive S-waves. Seismometers near the epicentre detect the P-waves and a computer can send an alert that arrives seconds to tens of seconds before the strong shaking. Japan's system stops bullet trains and opens fire-station doors automatically; Indonesia's BMKG is expanding a similar system.` },
  { t: `Why Indonesia has two monsoons`, b: `From about December to March, winds blow from Asia across the South China Sea, picking up moisture and bringing the west monsoon's heavy rain. From about June to September, dry winds blow from Australia, bringing the east monsoon and the dry season, especially to Nusa Tenggara, which is closest to Australia's deserts.` },
  { t: `El Niño brings drought to Indonesia`, b: `During an El Niño, the warmest water in the Pacific shifts east towards South America, and the rising air and rain move with it. Indonesia then often gets a longer, drier dry season, raising the risk of forest fires and haze, as in 1997 and 2015. La Niña does the opposite, bringing extra rain and floods.` },
  { t: `The Indian Ocean has its own El Niño`, b: `The Indian Ocean Dipole is a see-saw in sea surface temperature between the western Indian Ocean and the waters off Sumatra and Java. In a "positive" phase the water near Indonesia is cooler than usual, reducing rain in Indonesia and Australia and increasing it in East Africa. It was only recognised by scientists in 1999.` },
  { t: `Peat swamps store huge amounts of carbon`, b: `Indonesia's tropical peatlands, mainly in Sumatra, Kalimantan and Papua, are made of waterlogged plant remains that built up over thousands of years, sometimes more than 10 metres deep. They store enormous amounts of carbon. When they are drained for plantations they dry out and can burn underground for weeks, releasing smoke and CO₂.` },
  { t: `Indonesia's rainforests are among the world's largest`, b: `Indonesia has the third-largest area of tropical rainforest in the world, after Brazil and the Democratic Republic of Congo. Although it covers only about 1.3% of the Earth's land, it is home to roughly 10% of known flowering plant species, 12% of mammals and 17% of bird species.` },
  { t: `Orangutans live only in Sumatra and Borneo`, b: `The three species of orangutan, Bornean, Sumatran and Tapanuli, are found only in Indonesia and Malaysia. The Tapanuli orangutan, described in 2017 from the Batang Toru forest in North Sumatra, is the rarest great ape on Earth, with fewer than about 800 individuals. Orangutans spread seeds and help keep rainforests healthy.` },
  { t: `The world's largest flower grows in Sumatra`, b: `Rafflesia arnoldii, found in the rainforests of Sumatra and Borneo, produces the largest single flower in the world, up to about 1 metre across and 10 kg. It has no leaves, stems or roots of its own: it is a parasite living inside a jungle vine. It smells of rotting meat to attract carrion flies, which pollinate it.` },
  { t: `Birds of paradise`, b: `New Guinea, including Indonesia's Papua, is home to most of the world's birds of paradise, famous for their spectacular plumage and dances. Early European traders received skins with the feet removed, so people believed the birds never landed and lived in paradise, hence the name. Alfred Russel Wallace described several species in the 1850s.` },
  { t: `The Javan rhino survives in one place`, b: `The Javan rhinoceros once lived across Southeast Asia, but today the only population is in Ujung Kulon National Park at the western tip of Java, with fewer than 100 animals. It is one of the rarest large mammals in the world. Ujung Kulon became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1991.` },
  { t: `Subak, Bali's water temples`, b: `Bali's rice terraces are watered by subak, a system dating back at least to the 9th century in which farmers share water from springs and rivers through canals governed by water temples. The temples coordinate planting times so that pests are controlled across whole valleys. The cultural landscape of Bali's subak became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2012.` },
  { t: `Sea nomads of Southeast Asia`, b: `The Bajau people have lived at sea for centuries, in boats and stilt villages across Sulawesi, Borneo and the southern Philippines. Studies in 2018 found that many Bajau have enlarged spleens, a genetic adaptation that helps them dive for minutes at a time, free-diving to depths of 70 metres to hunt fish.` },
  { t: `Phinisi: the ships of Sulawesi`, b: `The phinisi, a two-masted wooden sailing ship, is built by Bugis and Konjo craftsmen in Bulukumba, South Sulawesi, without detailed written plans, passing knowledge between generations. The art of phinisi boatbuilding was listed by UNESCO as Intangible Cultural Heritage in 2017. Bugis sailors traded across the archipelago for centuries.` },
  { t: `The Spice Islands changed world history`, b: `Until the 1700s, cloves grew only in the northern Maluku islands and nutmeg only in the tiny Banda Islands. European powers fought for control of these spices, which were worth more than gold by weight in Europe. In 1667 the Dutch traded the island of Manhattan to the English partly to keep the Banda island of Run.` },
  { t: `The Strait of Malacca, a global highway`, b: `The Strait of Malacca, between Sumatra and the Malay Peninsula, is one of the busiest shipping lanes in the world, used by tens of thousands of ships a year carrying a large share of world trade, including much of the oil bound for China, Japan and Korea. At its narrowest, near Singapore, the Phillip Channel is under 3 km wide.` },
  { t: `Indonesia's "longest" and "highest"`, b: `The Kapuas in West Kalimantan, about 1,140 km long, is Indonesia's longest river and one of the longest island rivers in the world. Lake Toba is the largest lake, Puncak Jaya the highest peak, and Lake Matano in South Sulawesi, about 590 m deep, is the deepest lake in Southeast Asia.` },
  { t: `Jellyfish that do not sting`, b: `Kakaban Island in East Kalimantan has a lake cut off from the sea for thousands of years, where millions of jellyfish evolved almost no sting because they had no predators. It is one of only a few such lakes in the world; the others include Jellyfish Lake in Palau.` },
  { t: `Pink beaches and a pink sand island`, b: `Pink Beach on Padar and Komodo islands gets its colour from fragments of red foraminifera, tiny single-celled organisms with red shells, mixed with white coral sand. Only a few beaches in the world have naturally pink sand.` },
  { t: `The equator crosses Indonesia`, b: `The equator crosses Indonesia through several provinces, including West Sumatra and Riau on Sumatra, West and East Kalimantan, Central Sulawesi and Gorontalo, and the island of Halmahera in North Maluku. It also passes through the new capital region, Nusantara, which lies just south of the equator.` },
  { t: `Why Indonesian days are always about 12 hours`, b: `Near the equator, the Sun rises and sets at almost the same times all year, around 6 a.m. and 6 p.m., because the length of day changes very little with the seasons. In Jakarta the longest and shortest days differ by less than an hour. That is one reason Indonesia does not use daylight saving time.` },
  { t: `A country with no rivers`, b: `Several countries have no permanent rivers at all, including Saudi Arabia, Kuwait, Qatar, Oman, Bahrain, the United Arab Emirates, Yemen, Libya, Malta, Monaco and Vatican City. Saudi Arabia gets much of its drinking water from desalination plants that remove salt from seawater.` },
  { t: `The Nile is shared by eleven countries`, b: `The Nile basin covers parts of eleven countries, and about 300 million people depend on its water. Ethiopia's Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam on the Blue Nile, the largest hydroelectric dam in Africa, has caused long negotiations with Egypt and Sudan downstream, showing how shared rivers become geopolitical issues.` },
  { t: `The Aral Sea almost disappeared`, b: `The Aral Sea in Central Asia was once the fourth-largest lake in the world. From the 1960s, the Soviet Union diverted its two feeder rivers to irrigate cotton, and by the 2010s most of the lake had dried up, leaving ships stranded in a salty desert. Kazakhstan built a dam in 2005 that has partly restored the northern part.` },
  { t: `The Three Gorges Dam slowed the Earth slightly`, b: `China's Three Gorges Dam on the Yangtze is the world's largest power station by capacity, at about 22,500 megawatts. Its reservoir holds about 39 billion cubic metres of water; NASA scientists calculated that raising so much mass lengthens the day by about 0.06 microseconds.` },
  { t: `Iceland runs on volcanoes`, b: `Iceland sits on a hotspot on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, and almost all its electricity comes from hydropower and geothermal energy. About 90% of homes are heated by geothermal water piped from underground. Iceland also has about 30 active volcanic systems, and eruptions in 2010 at Eyjafjallajökull grounded flights across Europe for days.` },
  { t: `Hawaii's islands are a conveyor belt`, b: `The Hawaiian islands formed as the Pacific Plate moved north-west over a fixed hotspot deep in the mantle. The oldest islands in the chain are in the north-west and have eroded to atolls; the Big Island in the south-east is youngest and still growing, and a new volcano, Kamaʻehuakanaloa (formerly Lōʻihi), is rising under the sea beyond it.` },
  { t: `Atolls are rings left by sinking volcanoes`, b: `Charles Darwin proposed in 1842 that atolls form when a coral reef grows around a volcanic island that slowly sinks: the reef keeps growing upward while the island disappears, leaving a ring of coral around a lagoon. Drilling at Enewetak Atoll in 1952 hit volcanic rock more than 1,200 m below the coral, proving him right.` },
  { t: `Deserts can be cold`, b: `The Gobi Desert in Mongolia and China is a cold desert: winter temperatures fall below −40 °C, and it often snows. What makes a desert is dryness, usually less than 250 mm of precipitation a year. The Gobi is also one of the richest dinosaur-fossil areas in the world, where the first dinosaur eggs recognised by science were found in the 1920s.` },
  { t: `The Amazon rainforest is fed by the Sahara`, b: `Every year winds carry millions of tonnes of dust from the Sahara across the Atlantic. NASA satellite studies estimate that about 22,000 tonnes of phosphorus in this dust land on the Amazon each year, roughly replacing the phosphorus washed out of its poor soils by rain. One desert helps fertilise a rainforest on another continent.` },
  { t: `Mountains make their own weather`, b: `When moist air meets a mountain range, it is forced up, cools and drops rain on the windward side. The far side, in the "rain shadow", stays dry. That is why the north coast of Java and the southern slopes of some islands in Nusa Tenggara can differ sharply in rainfall, and why the Atacama Desert lies beside the Andes.` },
  { t: `The Himalayas hold the "third pole"`, b: `The glaciers of the Himalayas, Karakoram and Tibetan Plateau hold more ice than anywhere outside the polar regions, so they are called the "third pole". They feed rivers such as the Indus, Ganges, Brahmaputra, Yangtze and Mekong, on which nearly 2 billion people depend for water, food and electricity.` },
  { t: `Sea level is rising faster`, b: `Global average sea level rose about 20 cm between 1900 and 2020, and the rate has more than doubled since the 1990s to over 4 mm per year, mainly because warming water expands and glaciers and ice sheets melt. Low-lying coastal cities, from Jakarta to Semarang and Pekalongan, face flooding from both rising seas and sinking land.` },
  { t: `Coral reefs are underwater rainforests`, b: `Coral reefs cover less than 1% of the ocean floor but support around a quarter of all marine species. Reef-building corals live in partnership with algae that give them food and colour; when water gets too warm, corals expel the algae and turn white, a process called bleaching. Indonesia has about 18% of the world's coral reefs.` },
  { t: `Seagrass meadows are carbon champions`, b: `Seagrass meadows cover only a tiny part of the sea floor, yet they store carbon up to 35 times faster than tropical rainforests per unit area and hold much of it in their sediments for thousands of years. Indonesia has one of the largest areas of seagrass in the world, feeding dugongs and green turtles.` },
  { t: `Plastic in the ocean`, b: `Millions of tonnes of plastic enter the ocean every year, much of it through rivers in Asia. Sunlight and waves break it into microplastics, which have been found from the deepest trenches to Arctic ice and in fish and salt. Indonesia has pledged to cut marine plastic debris by 70%, and cities such as Bali and Jakarta have restricted single-use plastic bags.` },
  { t: `The Great Pacific Garbage Patch`, b: `Between Hawaii and California, ocean currents collect floating debris into a huge area known as the Great Pacific Garbage Patch, estimated at about 1.6 million km², around three times the size of France. It is not a solid island of rubbish but a soup of mostly small plastic pieces, some of which have been drifting for decades.` },
  { t: `Satellites map the whole Earth every few days`, b: `The Landsat programme has photographed the Earth's surface continuously since 1972, the longest record of its kind. Its images show forests being cleared, cities growing, glaciers shrinking and coastlines changing. The European Sentinel-2 satellites now photograph every land area on Earth about every five days, free for anyone to use.` },
  { t: `GPS was first a military system`, b: `The Global Positioning System was built by the US military from the 1970s and became fully available to civilians in 2000, when the deliberate error ("selective availability") was switched off, improving accuracy from about 100 m to under 10 m overnight. Today there are several systems, including Europe's Galileo, Russia's GLONASS and China's BeiDou.` },
  { t: `John Snow's cholera map`, b: `In 1854, during a cholera outbreak in London, Dr John Snow plotted the deaths on a street map and saw they clustered around one water pump on Broad Street. He persuaded officials to remove the pump handle. His map is often called the birth of spatial analysis and epidemiology, and it showed that cholera spreads through water, not bad air.` },
  { t: `The oldest known world map`, b: `The Babylonian "Imago Mundi", a clay tablet from about the 6th century BCE, shows the world as a flat disc with Babylon near the centre, surrounded by a ring of ocean. It is the oldest known map of the whole world. The first maps to use latitude and longitude were drawn from the work of Ptolemy, who listed about 8,000 places with coordinates around 150 CE.` },
  { t: `Why maps put north at the top`, b: `There is nothing natural about north being up. Medieval European maps often put east at the top, towards Jerusalem and the rising Sun, which is where the word "orientation" comes from. Some old Arab maps, such as al-Idrisi's famous map of 1154, put south at the top. The north-up convention spread with the magnetic compass and European navigation.` },
  { t: `The metre came from measuring the Earth`, b: `In 1791 the French Academy defined the metre as one ten-millionth of the distance from the North Pole to the equator along the meridian through Paris. Two astronomers, Delambre and Méchain, spent seven years surveying from Dunkirk to Barcelona to measure it. That is why the Earth's circumference is almost exactly 40,000 km.` },
  { t: `Indonesia's population is getting older`, b: `Indonesia is in a "demographic bonus": a large share of its people are of working age, which can boost the economy. But birth rates have fallen to about 2.1 children per woman, and the share of people over 60 is rising fast. By around 2045 Indonesia is expected to have many more elderly people, a challenge for health care and pensions.` },
  { t: `More than half of humanity lives in cities`, b: `In 2007, for the first time in history, more people lived in urban areas than in the countryside. The United Nations expects about two thirds of the world's population to be urban by 2050. In Indonesia, the urban share passed 50% around 2010 and keeps rising as people move to cities for work and education.` },
  { t: `Transmigration moved millions of people`, b: `From colonial times until the 2000s, Indonesia's transmigration programme moved millions of people from crowded Java, Bali and Madura to less populated islands such as Sumatra, Kalimantan, Sulawesi and Papua. It changed the population map of the country, created new farming towns, and also caused conflicts over land and forests.` },
  { t: `Mudik: one of the largest human migrations`, b: `Every year before Eid al-Fitr, tens of millions of Indonesians travel from cities back to their home villages in the tradition of mudik. The Ministry of Transportation estimated more than 190 million trips during the 2024 holiday. The flow shows how strongly Indonesian cities are linked to rural areas.` },
  { t: `Angel Falls is the highest waterfall`, b: `Angel Falls (Kerepakupai Merú) in Venezuela drops about 979 metres, with an uninterrupted fall of about 807 metres, the highest in the world. The water falls so far that much of it turns to mist before reaching the bottom. It is named after the American pilot Jimmie Angel, who flew over it in 1933.` },
  { t: `The biggest waterfall is under the sea`, b: `The Denmark Strait cataract, between Greenland and Iceland, is an underwater "waterfall": cold, dense water sinks about 3,500 metres down the sea floor. It carries around 3 million cubic metres of water per second, far more than any river waterfall on land.` },
  { t: `Lake Titicaca is the highest navigable lake`, b: `Lake Titicaca, on the border of Peru and Bolivia, lies about 3,812 metres above sea level and is often called the highest navigable lake in the world. The Uros people live on floating islands built from layers of totora reeds, which they must keep adding as the bottom layers rot.` },
  { t: `The world's biggest salt flat is a giant mirror`, b: `Salar de Uyuni in Bolivia is the largest salt flat on Earth, about 10,500 km². It is so flat, varying by less than a metre, that satellites use it to calibrate their instruments. After rain, a thin layer of water turns it into a vast mirror reflecting the sky. Beneath the salt lies one of the world's largest lithium reserves.` },
  { t: `The Caspian Sea is really a lake`, b: `The Caspian Sea, bordered by five countries, is the largest lake on Earth by area, about 371,000 km², larger than Germany. It is called a sea because it is salty (though less than the ocean) and very large. It has no outlet to the ocean, and its water level has been falling in recent decades.` },
  { t: `The driest desert can bloom`, b: `The Atacama Desert in Chile is one of the driest places on Earth. Yet rare storms can bring surprising results: in 2015, after unusual rains, parts of the desert burst into a carpet of flowers, the "desierto florido".` },
  { t: `Sand dunes can sing`, b: `In some deserts, sand dunes produce a deep, loud hum, sometimes lasting minutes, when sand avalanches down their slopes. The sound comes from millions of grains sliding in sync. Marco Polo wrote of "spirits" making music in the Gobi Desert, probably these singing dunes.` },
  { t: `Only a fifth of the Sahara is sand`, b: `Most people picture the Sahara as endless dunes, but only about 20–25% of it is sand sea (erg). Most of it is rocky plateaus (hamada), gravel plains and mountains. Its highest peak, Emi Koussi in Chad, is about 3,400 metres high, and snow has occasionally fallen in parts of the Sahara.` },
  { t: `The Great Rift Valley is splitting Africa`, b: `East Africa is slowly being pulled apart along the Great Rift Valley, at a few millimetres a year. In millions of years, the eastern part may break away to form a new continent, with a new ocean between. In Ethiopia's Afar region, a 60-km crack opened in about ten days in 2005.` },
  { t: `Iceland is growing`, b: `Iceland sits on the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, where the North American and Eurasian plates move apart about 2 cm a year. The island is slowly getting wider. At Þingvellir National Park you can walk through the rift between the plates, and at Silfra you can snorkel in it.` },
  { t: `A volcano was born in a cornfield`, b: `On 20 February 1943 a Mexican farmer, Dionisio Pulido, saw the ground in his cornfield crack and begin to smoke. Within a day a cone several metres high had formed; within a year it was over 300 metres tall. The volcano, Parícutin, erupted until 1952 and buried two villages. Scientists watched a volcano's whole life from birth.` },
  { t: `A new island appeared from the sea`, b: `In November 1963 an underwater eruption off Iceland built a brand-new island, Surtsey. Scientists have kept it almost untouched ever since to watch how life arrives: seeds carried by birds and waves, insects and eventually plants and nesting seabirds. Only scientists are allowed to visit.` },
  { t: `The Tonga eruption shook the whole planet`, b: `On 15 January 2022 the underwater volcano Hunga Tonga–Hunga Haʻapai exploded. Its pressure wave travelled around the world several times, its plume reached about 57 km, the highest ever recorded, and it caused small tsunamis across the Pacific. It also injected a huge amount of water vapour into the stratosphere.` },
  { t: `Pompeii was frozen in time`, b: `In 79 CE Mount Vesuvius buried the Roman towns of Pompeii and Herculaneum under ash and pyroclastic flows. Buried for more than 1,600 years, Pompeii preserved streets, bread in ovens and graffiti on walls. Pliny the Younger's eyewitness account gave the name "Plinian" to this type of explosive eruption.` },
  { t: `Supervolcano under Yellowstone`, b: `Yellowstone National Park in the USA sits on top of a huge volcanic system that has had three giant eruptions in the last 2.1 million years, the most recent about 640,000 years ago. Its heat powers more than 10,000 hydrothermal features, including Old Faithful, a geyser that erupts roughly every 60–110 minutes.` },
  { t: `Geysers need a special plumbing`, b: `A geyser needs hot rock, plenty of groundwater and narrow underground channels that trap water under pressure until it flashes into steam and erupts. These conditions are rare: about half the world's geysers are in Yellowstone. The word geyser comes from Geysir, a hot spring in Iceland.` },
  { t: `Earthquakes are measured by energy, not size`, b: `Each whole step on the moment magnitude scale means about 32 times more energy released. So a magnitude 9 earthquake releases about 1,000 times more energy than a magnitude 7. The largest ever recorded was the 1960 Valdivia earthquake in Chile, magnitude 9.5, which caused tsunamis as far as Japan.` },
  { t: `Animals that sense earthquakes`, b: `There are many stories of animals behaving strangely before earthquakes, from dogs howling to toads leaving ponds. Scientists have studied this for decades, but so far no animal signal has proved reliable enough to predict earthquakes. The most useful warning systems still detect the first, fast P-waves.` },
  { t: `Smong: a word that saved an island`, b: `When the 2004 tsunami hit Simeulue island off Aceh, very few of its residents died, although the island was close to the epicentre. Islanders had passed down a tradition, through stories and songs, called "smong": when a strong earthquake is followed by the sea pulling back, run to the hills. The tradition began after a tsunami in 1907.` },
  { t: `Tsunamis travel as fast as jet planes`, b: `In the deep ocean a tsunami can travel at over 700 km/h, the speed of a jet airliner, yet be less than a metre high, so ships barely notice it. As it reaches shallow water it slows down and its height grows dramatically. The first sign on shore is often the sea retreating unusually far.` },
  { t: `The tallest wave ever recorded was in a bay`, b: `In 1958 an earthquake in Lituya Bay, Alaska, triggered a rockslide into the narrow bay. The water surged up the opposite mountainside to a height of about 524 metres, stripping away trees. It is the largest wave run-up ever recorded. A fishing boat inside the bay rode the wave and survived.` },
  { t: `Rogue waves are real`, b: `Sailors long told stories of giant waves appearing from nowhere, which scientists doubted. On 1 January 1995 instruments on the Draupner oil platform in the North Sea recorded a single wave 25.6 metres high among waves around 12 metres. Satellites have since shown that such rogue waves are more common than once thought.` },
  { t: `The Gulf Stream keeps Europe mild`, b: `The Gulf Stream and its extension carry warm water from the Gulf of Mexico across the Atlantic. Together with prevailing westerly winds, this helps make western Europe much milder than places at the same latitude in Canada. London is farther north than Calgary, yet its winters are much warmer.` },
  { t: `The ocean's great conveyor belt`, b: `Cold, salty water sinks near Greenland and Antarctica and flows slowly along the deep ocean floor, rising again elsewhere. This global "thermohaline circulation" links all the oceans. A given parcel of water may take around 1,000 years to complete the loop. It moves heat, nutrients and carbon around the planet.` },
  { t: `Rubber ducks mapped ocean currents`, b: `In January 1992 a container of about 28,800 plastic bath toys, including yellow ducks, fell off a ship in the North Pacific. Over the following years they washed up in Alaska, Hawaii and South America, and some drifted into the Arctic. Oceanographer Curtis Ebbesmeyer used the "Friendly Floatees" to study ocean currents.` },
  { t: `Upwelling feeds the richest fisheries`, b: `Along the coasts of Peru, California and Namibia, winds push surface water offshore and cold, nutrient-rich water rises from below. These upwelling zones cover a tiny fraction of the ocean but produce a large share of the world's fish catch. Peru's anchoveta fishery is one of the largest on Earth.` },
  { t: `Banda Sea upwelling brings tuna`, b: `Indonesia also has seasonal upwelling. During the southeast monsoon, from about June to September, winds bring cool, nutrient-rich water up in the Banda and Arafura seas and south of Java. Plankton blooms follow, attracting fish such as tuna, which is why fishing seasons in eastern Indonesia follow the monsoon.` },
  { t: `Why the Red Sea is red`, b: `The Red Sea may be named for occasional blooms of a red-brown algae (Trichodesmium) near its surface, for the reddish mountains along its coast, or for an ancient system in which colours represented directions, with red meaning south. Nobody is sure which explanation is right.` },
  { t: `The Black Sea has a dead zone`, b: `Below about 150–200 metres, the Black Sea has almost no oxygen, and it is full of hydrogen sulfide. It is the largest body of such water on Earth. Because there is little oxygen to support decay, ancient shipwrecks on its floor are remarkably preserved, including a Greek ship about 2,400 years old found in 2017.` },
  { t: `The Mediterranean once dried up`, b: `About 6 million years ago the Strait of Gibraltar closed and the Mediterranean Sea largely evaporated, leaving thick layers of salt. Around 5.3 million years ago the Atlantic broke through, probably refilling the basin within a few years or less in the "Zanclean flood", one of the largest floods in Earth's history.` },
  { t: `The Channel was once dry land`, b: `During the last ice age, sea levels were about 120 metres lower, and Britain was joined to mainland Europe by a region now called Doggerland. People lived and hunted there. Rising seas drowned it around 8,000 years ago, and fishing boats still sometimes dredge up its stone tools and mammoth bones.` },
  { t: `Humans walked to America`, b: `During the last ice age, a land bridge called Beringia joined Siberia and Alaska where the Bering Strait is today. Most scientists think the first people to reach the Americas crossed this region at least 15,000 years ago, perhaps travelling along the coast. The strait is only about 82 km wide today.` },
  { t: `Two islands, two days`, b: `In the middle of the Bering Strait lie Big Diomede (Russia) and Little Diomede (USA), only about 4 km apart. The International Date Line runs between them, so Big Diomede is almost a full day ahead: they are nicknamed "Tomorrow Island" and "Yesterday Island".` },
  { t: `Samoa skipped a day`, b: `At the end of 29 December 2011, Samoa moved to the other side of the International Date Line to align its working week with Australia and New Zealand, its main trading partners. The country went straight to 31 December, so 30 December 2011 never happened there.` },
  { t: `Why Indonesia has three time zones`, b: `Indonesia stretches about 46° of longitude, so it uses three time zones: WIB (UTC+7), WITA (UTC+8) and WIT (UTC+9). The current division dates from 1988. When it is 7 a.m. in Jakarta, it is already 9 a.m. in Jayapura. China, by comparison, is even wider from east to west but uses just one time zone.` },
  { t: `The Prime Meridian was chosen by vote`, b: `In 1884, delegates from 25 countries met in Washington, D.C., and voted to make the meridian through Greenwich, London, the Prime Meridian (0° longitude). One reason was that most of the world's shipping already used charts based on Greenwich. France abstained and used the Paris meridian for decades more.` },
  { t: `Finding longitude needed a clock`, b: `Sailors could find latitude from the Sun or stars, but longitude requires knowing the exact time at home. In 1714 Britain offered a prize for a solution. The carpenter-clockmaker John Harrison built increasingly accurate sea clocks, and his H4 watch lost only about 5 seconds on a voyage to Jamaica in 1761–62.` },
  { t: `Polynesian navigators read the waves`, b: `Polynesian voyagers settled islands across a vast triangle from Hawaii to New Zealand to Easter Island without compasses or charts. They navigated by stars, swell patterns, clouds and birds. In 1976 the double-hulled canoe Hōkūleʻa sailed from Hawaii to Tahiti using these traditional methods, proving they worked.` },
  { t: `Austronesian sailors reached Madagascar`, b: `The Austronesian peoples, whose languages include Indonesian, Malay, Javanese and Tagalog, were among the greatest seafarers in history. Speakers of a language related to those of southern Borneo reached Madagascar, off Africa, over a thousand years ago. Malagasy, the language of Madagascar, is still an Austronesian language.` },
  { t: `The Borobudur ship sailed to Africa`, b: `Carvings on Borobudur show large outrigger ships from about the 8th–9th century. In 2003–04 a reconstruction based on these reliefs, the Samudra Raksa, sailed from Jakarta across the Indian Ocean to Ghana, retracing the ancient "Cinnamon Route". The ship is now displayed in a museum near Borobudur.` },
  { t: `Srivijaya ruled the straits`, b: `From about the 7th to the 13th century, the maritime kingdom of Srivijaya, based around Palembang in Sumatra, controlled trade through the Strait of Malacca and Sunda Strait. The Chinese monk Yijing stayed there in 671 to study Sanskrit and Buddhism, and wrote that over a thousand monks lived in the city.` },
  { t: `Majapahit and the archipelago`, b: `The Majapahit kingdom, based in East Java from 1293 to around the early 1500s, was one of the largest states in Southeast Asian history. The 1365 poem Nagarakretagama lists places across much of today's Indonesia as its tributaries. Its prime minister Gajah Mada is remembered for his oath to unite the archipelago (Sumpah Palapa).` },
  { t: `Indonesia's capital is moving`, b: `In 2019 Indonesia announced plans to move its capital from Jakarta, which is crowded and sinking, to a new city called Nusantara in East Kalimantan. Construction began in 2022. The site was chosen partly because it is near the geographical centre of the country and has relatively low risk of earthquakes and volcanoes.` },
  { t: `Countries that moved their capitals`, b: `Indonesia is not the first to build a new capital. Brazil moved its capital from Rio de Janeiro to the purpose-built Brasília in 1960, Nigeria from Lagos to Abuja in 1991, Myanmar to Naypyidaw in 2005, and Australia built Canberra as a compromise between Sydney and Melbourne.` },
  { t: `Lorentz: from glaciers to the sea`, b: `Lorentz National Park in Papua is the largest protected area in Southeast Asia, about 2.35 million hectares. It is the only protected area in the world that spans an unbroken sweep from snow-capped mountains to a tropical marine environment, including mangroves and lowland rainforest. It is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.` },
  { t: `Whale sharks gather in Cenderawasih Bay`, b: `In Cenderawasih Bay, Papua, whale sharks, the largest fish in the world, gather around fishermen's floating platforms (bagan) to feed on small fish. The bay is a national park and one of the few places where whale sharks can be seen almost all year round.` },
  { t: `The Lesser Sunda Islands are much drier`, b: `Nusa Tenggara, east of Bali, is much drier than Sumatra or Kalimantan. Hot, dry winds from Australia during the east monsoon bring a long dry season, and parts of Sumba and Timor get less than 1,000 mm of rain a year. The landscape there is savanna, with grasslands and horses, more like Australia than a rainforest.` },
  { t: `Bogor, the rain city`, b: `Bogor in West Java is nicknamed "Kota Hujan", the rain city, receiving roughly 3,500–4,000 mm of rain a year, with thunderstorms on many afternoons. The Bogor Botanical Gardens, founded in 1817, are among the oldest botanical gardens in Asia and have been important for tropical plant research.` },
  { t: `Afternoon thunderstorms follow the sun`, b: `In much of Indonesia, mornings are sunny and heavy rain comes in the afternoon or evening. The sun heats the land, warm moist air rises, cools and forms tall cumulonimbus clouds by mid-afternoon. Over the sea, the pattern is often reversed, with more rain at night and early morning.` },
  { t: `Land and sea breezes`, b: `By day, land heats up faster than the sea, so air rises over the land and cooler sea air flows in: a sea breeze. At night, land cools faster, and the breeze blows from land to sea. Traditional fishermen along Indonesia's coasts have long used the land breeze to sail out before dawn and the sea breeze to return.` },
  { t: `Föhn winds: hot, dry mountain winds`, b: `When moist air crosses a mountain range, it cools and drops its rain on the windward side, then warms quickly as it descends the other side, becoming hot and dry. These winds are called föhn in the Alps. In Indonesia similar winds have local names, such as angin gending in Probolinggo and angin kumbang in Cirebon and Brebes.` },
  { t: `The Dieng Plateau gets frost`, b: `The Dieng Plateau in Central Java, about 2,000 metres high, sometimes gets frost on clear nights in the dry season, around July and August, when temperatures can drop close to or below 0 °C. Locals call it "embun upas", poisonous dew, because it damages potato crops.` },
  { t: `Tropical soils are often poor`, b: `Rainforests look incredibly fertile, but their soils are often thin and poor, because heavy rain washes nutrients away and most nutrients are held in the living plants. When forest is cleared, the soil can lose fertility within a few years. Java is an exception: its volcanic ash soils are very rich, which helps explain its dense population.` },
  { t: `Rice terraces are engineered landscapes`, b: `Terraced rice fields in Bali, Java and the Philippines turn steep slopes into flat, flooded steps that hold water and soil. Terraces reduce erosion and let water flow gently from field to field. The Banaue terraces in the Philippines were built by hand more than 2,000 years ago according to local tradition, though some researchers argue they are younger.` },
  { t: `Floating markets on Kalimantan's rivers`, b: `In Kalimantan, rivers have long been the main roads. At the floating markets of Banjarmasin, such as Lok Baintan, traders sell fruit, vegetables and fish from small boats (jukung) in the early morning. Many houses are built on stilts or rafts along the river, adapted to rising and falling water levels.` },
  { t: `Rinjani's crater lake`, b: `Mount Rinjani on Lombok, about 3,726 metres, is Indonesia's second-highest volcano. Its caldera holds a crescent-shaped lake, Segara Anak, and a young volcanic cone, Gunung Barujari, growing inside it. The caldera formed in the great eruption of Samalas in 1257, one of the largest of the last 2,000 years.` },
  { t: `Karst landscapes of Indonesia`, b: `Limestone dissolved by rainwater forms karst: sinkholes, caves, underground rivers and cone-shaped hills. Indonesia has spectacular karst in Gunung Sewu in Java, Maros-Pangkep in Sulawesi and Sangkulirang-Mangkalihat in Kalimantan. Karst areas store huge amounts of groundwater and often hide ancient cave art.` },
  { t: `Deforestation seen from space`, b: `Satellite images, such as those from the Landsat programme since 1972, let scientists track forest loss worldwide. Global Forest Watch uses them to publish near-real-time alerts. Studies show Indonesia's rate of primary forest loss has fallen significantly since its peak around 2016, though it has not stopped.` },
  { t: `The haze that crosses borders`, b: `When peatland and forest fires burn in Sumatra and Kalimantan during dry years, especially El Niño years such as 1997 and 2015, smoke haze can spread to Singapore, Malaysia and beyond. The 2015 haze affected tens of millions of people. Peat fires are hard to put out because they smoulder underground.` },
  { t: `Night lights map the world's economy`, b: `Satellite photos of Earth at night show where people live and how much electricity they use. Economists use changes in night-time lights to estimate economic growth where statistics are poor. In images of Indonesia, Java glows like a continuous string of lights, while much of Papua is dark.` },
  { t: `The Earth has more trees than stars in the Milky Way`, b: `A 2015 study using satellite data and ground measurements estimated there are about 3 trillion trees on Earth, more than the estimated 100–400 billion stars in our galaxy. The same study estimated that the number of trees has fallen by nearly half since the start of human civilisation.` },
  { t: `The Amazon has thousands of tree species`, b: `Scientists estimate the Amazon has around 16,000 tree species and about 390 billion individual trees. But just 227 "hyperdominant" species, such as the açaí palm and Brazil nut, make up half of all the trees. Many rare species have never been formally described.` },
  { t: `The Congo is the deepest river`, b: `The Congo River is the deepest river in the world, with depths measured at over 220 metres in its lower rapids. It is the second-largest river by discharge after the Amazon and crosses the equator twice. Its rapids are so fierce that fish on either side have evolved into different species.` },
  { t: `A river that flows backwards`, b: `The Tonlé Sap River in Cambodia reverses direction twice a year. In the rainy season, the swollen Mekong pushes water up the river into the Tonlé Sap Lake, which grows up to about five times in area. In the dry season, the river flows back out. This rhythm supports one of the world's largest freshwater fisheries.` },
  { t: `The Mekong's giant fish`, b: `The Mekong River is home to some of the world's largest freshwater fish, including the Mekong giant catfish and the giant freshwater stingray. In 2022 fishermen in Cambodia caught a giant stingray weighing about 300 kg, believed to be the largest freshwater fish ever recorded. It was released back into the river.` },
  { t: `The Great Lakes are an inland sea`, b: `The five Great Lakes of North America together hold about a fifth of the world's surface fresh water. Lake Superior alone is the largest freshwater lake by area, about 82,000 km². The lakes are so big that they have tides (very small ones), storms with huge waves and thousands of shipwrecks.` },
  { t: `Niagara Falls was once switched off`, b: `In 1969 engineers built a temporary dam to divert the Niagara River away from the American Falls for several months so they could study erosion and loose rock. The dry cliff revealed coins and other objects. In 1848, ice jams upstream had already stopped the falls for about a day.` },
  { t: `The Grand Canyon shows 2 billion years of rock`, b: `The Colorado River has cut the Grand Canyon up to about 1,800 metres deep. Its walls expose rock layers from nearly 2 billion years ago at the bottom to about 270 million years ago at the rim, like pages in a history book. The canyon itself was carved within roughly the last 5–6 million years.` },
  { t: `Uluru is the tip of a buried rock`, b: `Uluru in central Australia rises about 348 metres above the plain, but most of the rock formation lies underground. It is made of sandstone layers tilted almost vertical. For the Aṉangu people it is a sacred site, and climbing it was permanently banned in October 2019.` },
  { t: `New Zealand is mostly underwater`, b: `New Zealand is the visible part of Zealandia, a mostly submerged continent of about 4.9 million km². Around 94% of it lies under the ocean. In 2017 geologists argued it meets all the criteria of a continent, making it the world's smallest, thinnest and youngest one.` },
  { t: `Antarctica has hidden lakes and mountains`, b: `Under Antarctica's ice, up to about 4.8 km thick, lie more than 400 lakes, the largest being Lake Vostok, and a mountain range as large as the Alps, the Gamburtsev Mountains, which no one has ever seen.` },
  { t: `Antarctica has a waterfall of "blood"`, b: `At Taylor Glacier in Antarctica, a stream of rust-red water flows out of the ice onto a frozen lake. The water comes from a very salty, oxygen-poor pool trapped beneath the glacier for more than a million years; it is rich in iron, which turns red when it meets air. Microbes live in it without sunlight.` },
  { t: `Antarctica belongs to no one`, b: `The Antarctic Treaty, signed in 1959 by 12 countries and now joined by over 50, sets Antarctica aside for peace and science. Military activity and mining are banned, and territorial claims are frozen. Around 1,000 to 5,000 people live there at research stations, depending on the season.` },
  { t: `The Arctic is an ocean, Antarctica a continent`, b: `The North Pole lies in the middle of the Arctic Ocean, covered by floating sea ice a few metres thick. The South Pole is on a continent covered by an ice sheet about 2.7 km thick at the pole. That is one reason the South Pole is much colder: its average temperature is about −49 °C, compared with about −18 °C at the North Pole.` },
  { t: `Polar bears and penguins never meet`, b: `Polar bears live only in the Arctic and penguins live almost entirely in the Southern Hemisphere, so they never meet in the wild. The only penguin that lives north of the equator is the Galápagos penguin, and only just: some live on islands that straddle the equator.` },
  { t: `Svalbard's seed vault`, b: `Deep inside a mountain on the Norwegian island of Spitsbergen, the Svalbard Global Seed Vault, opened in 2008, stores backup copies of over a million seed samples from gene banks around the world. The permafrost keeps it cold even if power fails. It was used for the first time in 2015 after war damaged a gene bank in Syria.` },
  { t: `Monaco is the most densely populated country`, b: `Monaco, on the Mediterranean coast, covers only about 2 km² but has about 38,000 residents, a density of around 19,000 people per km². Among larger countries, Bangladesh is the most crowded, with over 170 million people in an area about a third the size of Sumatra.` },
  { t: `Mongolia is the least densely populated country`, b: `Mongolia has about 3.5 million people in 1.56 million km², only around 2 people per km², the lowest of any sovereign country. About half the population lives in the capital, Ulaanbaatar, while many others are herders who move with their animals and live in round felt tents called gers.` },
  { t: `Some countries are shrinking`, b: `Populations in Japan, South Korea, Italy and many Eastern European countries are falling, as birth rates are low and people live longer. South Korea's fertility rate of about 0.7 children per woman in 2023 was the lowest in the world.` },
  { t: `Where four countries almost meet`, b: `Most borders meet at tripoints, but the Four Corners in the USA is where four states meet at right angles. Between countries, a true quadripoint is extremely rare. In Africa, Zambia, Botswana, Zimbabwe and Namibia come very close at the Zambezi, where Zambia and Botswana share a border only about 150 metres long.` },
  { t: `A town divided between two countries`, b: `Baarle-Hertog (Belgium) and Baarle-Nassau (Netherlands) form one town made of more than 20 Belgian enclaves inside the Netherlands, and Dutch enclaves inside those. The border runs through shops, gardens and even houses; a building's nationality is traditionally decided by where its front door is.` },
  { t: `Countries with the most neighbours`, b: `China and Russia each share land borders with 14 countries, the most in the world. Indonesia shares land borders with three: Malaysia on Borneo, Papua New Guinea on New Guinea and Timor-Leste on Timor. It also shares maritime boundaries with ten countries.` }
];
